    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. Here's what I changed to satisfy the new `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` customer-code format:\n\n- **`CustomerCode.java`** \u2014 the single formatting definition. `format` now takes `(city, lastName, sequence)` and emits `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`. Since both prefixes are \"upper-cased first three letters,\" I extracted a private `prefix(value)` helper so that rule lives in one place rather than being duplicated for city and last name.\n\n- **`SameCity.java`** (new) \u2014 a small membership helper mirroring the existing `Namesake`/`Household` pattern: `matches(owner, city)`, case-insensitive with whitespace trimmed. Single responsibility: \"are two owners in the same city.\"\n\n- **`AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 now derives a *per-city* sequence (one more than the owners already in that city) using `SameCity.matches`, instead of a global owner count, then formats with city + last name.\n\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 no logic change needed: `membershipNumber` reads `owner.getCustomerCode()` directly, so it automatically rebuilds on the new format. I only refreshed the javadoc example (`SPR-SMI-0007-M26`) so the doc matches reality.\n\nThe membership number (the value derived from `customerCode`) is rebuilt purely by construction since it composes the code rather than parsing the old shape.",
